<p>Recent reporting has highlighted evidence that ChatGPT is building and using its own search infrastructure, including a search index referred to as Labrador in antitrust testimony and job postings. For SEO teams, the important shift is simple: ChatGPT visibility may depend on more than how a page performs in Google.</p>
<figure class="article-image"><img src="/assets/images/blog/chatgpt-labrador-search-index-seo-2026.svg" alt="Diagram showing a ChatGPT search index retrieving web pages for AI answers"></figure>
<h2>What the Labrador reports mean</h2><p>The reports describe an internal search index used to support ChatGPT search. The existence of a separate retrieval system does not mean Google SEO stops mattering. It means AI search can have its own discovery and retrieval path.</p><h2>Why SEOs should care</h2><p>A page can rank well in Google and still be difficult for an AI system to retrieve for a specific prompt. Conversely, a useful page may be surfaced in an AI answer even when it is not a top organic result.</p>
<h2>What to measure</h2><ul><li>Brand mentions in ChatGPT answers.</li><li>Cited URLs from your site.</li><li>Pages retrieved for important prompts.</li><li>Referral traffic from AI products.</li><li>Conversions from AI-referred users.</li></ul>
<h2>How to prepare content</h2><p>Keep important pages crawlable, useful and easy to understand. Use descriptive headings, clear entity names, original information and direct answers. Avoid creating thin pages only because a new AI search system exists.</p>
<h2>Build a prompt test set</h2><ol><li>List commercial questions customers ask.</li><li>Add comparison and problem-solving prompts.</li><li>Record cited sources and brand mentions.</li><li>Repeat the test monthly and after major AI search changes.</li><li>Compare retrieval with organic performance.</li></ol>
<h2>Do not chase citations blindly</h2><p>A citation is useful only when the underlying page satisfies the user. Improve the page first. Then use AI-search tracking to see whether the stronger page is retrieved and cited more often.</p>
<h2>How this fits with Google SEO</h2><p>Keep technical SEO, internal linking and strong content as the foundation. Add AI-search measurement on top. You do not need two completely separate content strategies for every search engine.</p>
<h2>Related ToolBoxKart guides</h2><p>For ChatGPT workflows, read <a href="/blog/chatgpt-data-plugin-work-codex-guide">the ChatGPT Data Plugin guide</a>. For cross-model work, see <a href="/blog/claude-vs-chatgpt-vs-gemini-seo-coding">Claude vs ChatGPT vs Gemini</a>. For AI visibility, use <a href="/blog/google-ai-search-visibility-report">the Google AI Search Visibility Report</a>. For prompt measurement, read <a href="/blog/search-console-ai-performance-report-guide">the Search Console AI Performance Report guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Is Labrador a public search engine?</h3><p>No. Reporting describes it as internal search infrastructure associated with ChatGPT search.</p><h3>Does this replace Google SEO?</h3><p>No. Google remains a major discovery channel, while AI search adds another visibility surface.</p><h3>What should SEOs track first?</h3><p>Start with a fixed prompt set, cited URLs, brand mentions and AI referral traffic.</p>
